<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uglich</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uglich is a town on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Long part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> lands, it became the seat of its own princes in 1218 and was later absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. It is best known as the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-uglich">Tsarevich Dmitry</a>, son of Ivan the Terrible, died in 1591, commemorated by the Church of St Dmitry on the Blood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uglich.jpg" alt="Uglich" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike1979 Russia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feodorovskaya_Church_Bogoyavlensky_Monastery_Uglich_2023-07-22_7446.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uglich lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga River</a> in Yaroslavl Oblast. It was first documented in 1148 as Ugliche Pole, meaning Corner Field. The name is thought to allude to a nearby turn in the Volga and derives from the Russian word ugol, a corner or nook.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1218 until 1328 Uglich was the seat of a small princedom, and the local princes then sold their rights to the great prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. As a border town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> it was burned several times in conflicts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and the grand prince of Tver. In 1462 Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of Moscow gave the town to his younger brother Andrey Bolshoy. Under Andrey the town was expanded and its first stone buildings were raised, among them a cathedral, the Intercession Monastery and the red-brick palace of the prince, completed in 1481 and still standing.</p><p>Under Ivan the Terrible the town passed to his only brother, Yury. Its inhabitants helped the tsar to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> by building a wooden fortress that was carried down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to the city. Uglich prospered throughout the 16th century, after which its fortunes declined.</p><p>After Ivan's death his youngest son, Dmitry Ivanovich, was banished to Uglich in 1584. On 15 May 1591 the ten-year-old boy was found dead with his throat cut in the palace courtyard. Suspicion fell at once on the tsar's chief adviser, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>, but official investigators concluded that the death was an accident. They also cut the tongue from the cathedral bell that had rung the news and sent it into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Dmitry was the last of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a>, and his death is seen as a precipitating cause of the dynastic and political crisis known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. People readily believed he was alive and supported several False Dmitrys, each of whom tried to seize the Muscovite throne. During that crisis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> besieged the Alexeievsky and Uleima monasteries and burned them, killing the people who had taken refuge inside.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> tsars soon had the boy canonised and made Uglich a place of pilgrimage. In 1690 the town built the small Church of St Demetrios on the Blood where he was thought to have been killed, and the palace became a museum. The image of the tsarevich with a knife in his hand was adopted as the town's coat of arms. The Assumption church of the Alexeyevsky Monastery, dated 1628, is regarded as a gem of medieval Russian architecture. Damming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the 20th century, under Stalin, caused severe flooding of the town's outskirts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uglich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
